[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Polymorphism in JavaScript"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism, derived from the Greek words 'poly' (many) and 'morph' (form), is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming (OOP). It allows objects of different classes to respond to the same method call in their own specific way. In JavaScript, polymorphism is achieved through various mechanisms, including inheritance, interfaces (using duck typing), and function overloading (though not directly supported in the same way as in some other languages)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Polymorphism through Inheritance"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inheritance is a key enabler of polymorphism. A subclass inherits properties and methods from its parent class. It can then override these methods to provide its own implementation, demonstrating polymorphic behavior."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Parent class
class Animal {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  makeSound() {
    console.log('Generic animal sound');
  }
}

// Child class 1
class Dog extends Animal {
  makeSound() {
    console.log('Woof!');
  }
}

// Child class 2
class Cat extends Animal {
  makeSound() {
    console.log('Meow!');
  }
}

const animal = new Animal('Generic Animal');
const dog = new Dog('Buddy');
const cat = new Cat('Whiskers');

animal.makeSound(); // Output: Generic animal sound
dog.makeSound();    // Output: Woof!
cat.makeSound();    // Output: Meow!"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, the `makeSound()` method is polymorphic. Each subclass (`Dog` and `Cat`) provides its own implementation of the method, resulting in different outputs when called on instances of those classes. The `animal` object uses the base class implementation."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Polymorphism through Duck Typing"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript is a dynamically typed language, which means that the type of a variable is checked at runtime. This allows for a form of polymorphism known as 'duck typing'. If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, then it is a duck. In other words, if an object has the methods and properties that are needed, it can be used regardless of its actual class."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"function makeSound(animal) {
  if (typeof animal.makeSound === 'function') {
    animal.makeSound();
  } else {
    console.log('This animal cannot make a sound.');
  }
}

const duck = {
  makeSound: function() {
    console.log('Quack!');
  }
};

const robot = {
  makeSound: function() {
    console.log('Beep boop!');
  }
};

makeSound(duck);  // Output: Quack!
makeSound(robot); // Output: Beep boop!

const inanimateObject = {};
makeSound(inanimateObject); // Output: This animal cannot make a sound."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `makeSound` function doesn't care about the specific class of the `animal` object. It only checks if the object has a `makeSound` method. If it does, the method is called. This demonstrates duck typing, a form of polymorphism where the type of an object is less important than its behavior."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Function Overloading (Simulated)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript doesn't directly support function overloading in the same way as languages like Java or C++. However, you can achieve similar behavior by using techniques like checking the number or type of arguments passed to a function and then executing different code blocks accordingly."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"function add(a, b) {
  if (typeof b === 'undefined') {
    return a + a; // If only one argument is provided, add it to itself
  } else {
    return a + b; // If two arguments are provided, add them together
  }
}

console.log(add(5));    // Output: 10
console.log(add(5, 3)); // Output: 8"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `add` function behaves differently depending on the number of arguments passed to it. This simulates function overloading, allowing the function to perform different actions based on the input."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Polymorphism"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code Reusability: Polymorphism allows you to write more generic code that can work with objects of different classes."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: It makes your code more adaptable to changes and new requirements. You can easily add new classes without modifying existing code."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintainability: Polymorphic code is often easier to understand and maintain because it is more modular and less tightly coupled."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism is a powerful tool in JavaScript that enhances code reusability, flexibility, and maintainability. By understanding and utilizing inheritance, duck typing, and simulated function overloading, you can write more robust and adaptable applications."}]
